            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has alot of air leakage associated with the following locations:



** The foundation walls &amp; header space.



** The older windows in the basement and on the main floor



** The attic access panel



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you can achieve about a 40% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 412 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house have been renovated on the interior to increase the stud cavity from a 2x4 to a 2x6 &amp; you insulated that cavity with R24 batt insulation. We discussed an opportunity for you to add another R10 to the exterior of these walls (under the new siding you are planning to install) and therefore qualifying to participate in the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).  I have modeled adding R10 (2" of extruded polystyrene rigid insulation), bringing the total R-value close to the minimum R34 that is just a bit higher than what is required for new construction in the NWT.



The basement walls are completely uninsulated. These basement walls include a lower uninsulated concrete section and an upper 2x6 pony wall that is also uninsulated. The header space above the basement is uninsulated. As discussed, there are 2 levels of rebates for basement insulation - $625 for adding R10 - R23, and $1,250 for adding R24+. There is another $250 for adding a minimum R20 to the basement header space. For participants of the DHERP, all of these basement insulation rebates are doubled !!!



You will insulate the basement pony walls &amp; header space with the same as the main floor walls - R24 batt inside &amp; 2" (R10) of rigid insulation on the outside. For the lower concrete walls, you could put 2" (R10) against the concrete and a 2x4 (R14 Roxul) stud wall, to achieve R24. 



Savings: By adding R10 to the exterior of the main floor walls, R34 to the basement pony walls and header space &amp;   R24 to the lower basement concrete walls, you could potentially save $ 2,112 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 88.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 6,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP):

- $3,000 for adding R10 to 100% of the main floor walls

- $2,500 for adding R34 to the basement pony walls &amp; header space and R24 to the lower concrete walls

- $   500 for adding R34 to the basement header space



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic insulation is currently about 6" (R20) on average. I have modeled the opportunity to increase this up to a minimum 15" (R50).



Savings: By increasing the attic insulation from R20 to R50, this is estimated to save $ 241 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.2 GJ, and qualify for a $ 750 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

Please see the Insulating Main Walls section of this report, as it incorporates the foundation wall insulation upgrade as well.</foundation>
                <windows>

You have previously updated the 3 windows in the front of the main floor, and there are still 6 older windows that can be upgraded. On the main floor, there is one on the right and 2 on the back. In the basement, there are 2 in the front and one in the back. I recommend you upgrade these 6 older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these 6 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 176 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.4 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a range hood       

        - a bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 0.6 Pascals (very low as your house currently has alot of air leakage, however this will change as you complete your renovations). As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your house does not have any chimney vented appliances.



During my assessment of your home, I did not see smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climah transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
